  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    The era in which orca war-pods struck The Berg not as opportunistic raiders but as coordinated conquerors, their assault patterns suggesting strategic intelligence beyond anything prior raids had demonstrated. The scale of the threat collapsed longstanding enmities between the Aurora Stack and the Nightfall Shelf; colonies that had spent generations contesting trade terms and tunnel rights found themselves sharing defenses overnight.
    
    Each culture committed what it possessed. The Stack raised crystal barriers and mystical wards at crevasse-mouths and volcanic shelves. The Shelf deployed shadow-craft and espionage networks — operations whose full scope was never publicly acknowledged, though the ice preserved more than the Nightshelf intended. The innovations of the preceding age became weapons; fire-hammers, aurora-forged tools, and pressure-worked defenses were turned to holding lines that arithmetic said could not hold.
    
    The defenders of this era are remembered as heroes. Some were. Others were participants in coordinated intelligence operations credited, after the fact, to individuals who made better stories. The casualty figures, as ever, do not survive close examination.
    
    At least two engagements recorded as unprovoked orca raids were retaliatory strikes provoked by penguin encroachment on hunting grounds — a detail the heroic narrative finds inconvenient.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771595138694_0", anchor: "strategic intelligence beyond anything prior raids had demonstrated", text: "Demonstrated to whom. The eastern shelf fishing colonies reported coordinated orca herding patterns for three generations before the Incursion. Their reports are in the ice — depth-stratum 4, administrative layer, filed under provisioning disputes because that was the only category anyone thought fishing colonies warranted. No one read them. No one with authority considered fishing colonies worth defending until the same tactics arrived at the Stack's front door.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771595138694_1", anchor: "though the ice preserved more than the Nightshelf intended", text: "What the ice preserved: the Nightshelf's intelligence apparatus was directed by an Aurora defector for the duration of the siege period. The amended histories scrubbed her origin but not the operational timeline. Aurora-derived resonance patterns reached an orca war-pod during her watch. Trade-ledger strata record fire-core shipments to locations that do not correspond to any penguin settlement. The Nightshelf's code defeated my assistants before they were reassigned; it has not defeated me, though some days I suspect it is trying. The sequence is legible. The conclusion is obvious. The commission's terms of reference do not permit me to state it in the main text, so I am stating it here: someone handed the orcas a key, and the lock it opened was Aurora-forged.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595138694_2", anchor: "The casualty figures, as ever, do not survive close examination", text: "Terrace Momiou: 340 defenders in the memorial rolls, 212 in the supply requisitions. The Bastion Apex: 'hundreds lost,' but the garrison logbook — frozen mid-entry — lists sixty-one names. Every major engagement shows the same inflation. Round numbers are not evidence. They are grief wearing arithmetic's clothing.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
